 "measurement_notes": "Recorded AFTER the measurement, during #12349 review, and deliberately not re-derived by hand: these are defects of the INSTRUMENT as it ran, disclosed rather than edited out of the numbers. (1) FIXTURE ORDERING \u2014 the two FalkorDB arms shared one graph, and `arm_falkordb` inserts the five #8933 fixture rows at the END of its own call (the harness gives each arm its own graph now, so a re-run measures both on 300), so the recovery arm's recall sweep ran over a 305-document index while the shipped arm's ran over 300 (`index_numDocuments` records both, unedited). The harness now gives each arm its own graph, so a re-run measures both on 300; the committed numbers predate that line. The DIRECTION is not rescued by this defect, and it is not PROVEN either (corrected during the #12349 review that added this note): five extra documents compete for the same top-3 window, which is plausibly the harder population, but they also move document frequencies and TF-IDF re-weighting can move a name up as well as down. What does not depend on that argument: the recovery arm is compared against the SHIPPED arm's own 300-document numbers and recorded zero regressions. A re-run is still the honest confirmation and is what #12350's repair should be measured with. (2) ENGINE STRING \u2014 `server_version` already renders as \"PostgreSQL 16.15 (Debian \u2026)\", and the harness prefixed another \"PostgreSQL\", so the two Postgres arms read \"PostgreSQL PostgreSQL 16.15\"; the harness is fixed, the recorded string is left as the run emitted it. (3) `population_file` is normalised to a repo-relative path here: the run emitted the authoring checkout's absolute path, which is a fact about one box rather than about the measurement. No number in either artifact changed.",
